1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,027
This is Egypt.

2
00:00:01,027 --> 00:00:03,902
It's roughly the size of France and Spain

3
00:00:03,902 --> 00:00:04,860
put together.

4
00:00:05,210 --> 00:00:07,116
And it's home to more than a hundred

5
00:00:07,116 --> 00:00:07,940
million people.

6
00:00:08,290 --> 00:00:09,806
But almost all of them,

7
00:00:09,806 --> 00:00:11,512
about ninety-five percent,

8
00:00:11,512 --> 00:00:13,408
live on this thin green line,

9
00:00:13,408 --> 00:00:16,251
which covers only around five percent of the

10
00:00:16,251 --> 00:00:16,820
country.

11
00:00:17,170 --> 00:00:20,416
So why would a hundred million people squeeze

12
00:00:20,416 --> 00:00:22,110
into such a tiny space?

13
00:00:25,060 --> 00:00:26,675
By the end of this video,

14
00:00:26,675 --> 00:00:29,160
you'll see why this strip gave birth to

15
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:31,520
one of the first great civilizations,

16
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:34,067
how Egypt moved an entire temple to save

17
00:00:34,067 --> 00:00:34,316
it,

18
00:00:34,316 --> 00:00:36,490
and why the river behind it all is

19
00:00:36,490 --> 00:00:38,540
now at the center of a conflict.

20
00:00:38,890 --> 00:00:40,427
Take away the Nile,

21
00:00:40,427 --> 00:00:43,269
and Egypt is almost entirely desert.

22
00:00:43,269 --> 00:00:46,650
The Sahara covers nearly the whole country.

23
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,576
And look at the borders.

24
00:00:48,576 --> 00:00:52,232
Egypt's western and southern borders are almost perfectly

25
00:00:52,232 --> 00:00:53,240
straight lines.

26
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:56,644
They were drawn in agreements between colonial powers

27
00:00:56,644 --> 00:00:59,102
along lines of longitude and latitude,

28
00:00:59,102 --> 00:01:01,434
and they could be drawn with a ruler

29
00:01:01,434 --> 00:01:03,640
because almost nobody lived there.

30
00:01:03,990 --> 00:01:07,496
Cairo gets only around twenty-five millimeters of rain

31
00:01:07,496 --> 00:01:08,707
in an entire year.

32
00:01:08,707 --> 00:01:11,320
London gets more than twenty times that.

33
00:01:11,670 --> 00:01:14,510
So picture Egypt as one hundred squares of

34
00:01:14,510 --> 00:01:14,906
land.

35
00:01:14,906 --> 00:01:17,613
Only about five of them are green enough

36
00:01:17,613 --> 00:01:19,000
to live on and farm.

37
00:01:19,350 --> 00:01:21,051
Now picture the people.

38
00:01:21,051 --> 00:01:24,880
About ninety-five out of every hundred Egyptians live

39
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:26,510
on those five squares.

40
00:01:26,860 --> 00:01:29,291
That makes the Nile Valley one of the

41
00:01:29,291 --> 00:01:31,786
most crowded farming regions on Earth,

42
00:01:31,786 --> 00:01:34,985
with roughly two thousand people for every square

43
00:01:34,985 --> 00:01:36,840
kilometer of habitable land.

44
00:01:39,790 --> 00:01:41,421
The answer is one river.

45
00:01:41,421 --> 00:01:42,073
The Nile,

46
00:01:42,073 --> 00:01:44,683
one of the longest rivers in the world,

47
00:01:44,683 --> 00:01:48,141
roughly six thousand six hundred kilometers from its

48
00:01:48,141 --> 00:01:50,620
farthest source to the Mediterranean.

49
00:01:50,970 --> 00:01:53,725
Whether it's the longest river on Earth depends

50
00:01:53,725 --> 00:01:54,643
on who you ask.

51
00:01:54,643 --> 00:01:56,939
Some measurements put the Amazon ahead.

52
00:01:56,939 --> 00:01:59,120
It all comes down to where you decide

53
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:00,440
a river really begins.

54
00:02:00,790 --> 00:02:02,659
And here's the strange part.

55
00:02:02,659 --> 00:02:05,558
Most of Egypt's water doesn't fall in Egypt.

56
00:02:05,558 --> 00:02:08,586
It falls thousands of kilometers to the south,

57
00:02:08,586 --> 00:02:11,486
in the highlands of Ethiopia and around Lake

58
00:02:11,486 --> 00:02:12,130
Victoria.

59
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:13,500
Every summer,

60
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:16,706
monsoon rains soak the Ethiopian Highlands.

61
00:02:16,706 --> 00:02:19,402
The water rushes down the Blue Nile,

62
00:02:19,402 --> 00:02:21,880
joins the White Nile at Khartoum,

63
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:22,608
in Sudan,

64
00:02:22,608 --> 00:02:23,847
and flows north,

65
00:02:23,847 --> 00:02:25,960
straight through the Sahara.

66
00:02:26,310 --> 00:02:26,472
That's why the Nile does something that looks

67
00:02:26,472 --> 00:02:26,547
impossible on a map.

68
00:02:26,547 --> 00:02:26,709
It crosses well over a thousand kilometers of

69
00:02:26,709 --> 00:02:26,879
desert without a single major river joining it,

70
00:02:26,879 --> 00:02:26,960
and without drying up.

71
00:02:27,310 --> 00:02:28,310
It isn't easy.

72
00:02:28,310 --> 00:02:29,643
In the desert heat,

73
00:02:29,643 --> 00:02:32,575
the river loses an enormous amount of water

74
00:02:32,575 --> 00:02:34,575
to evaporation along the way.

75
00:02:34,575 --> 00:02:36,774
Only a river fed by a huge rainy

76
00:02:36,774 --> 00:02:40,440
region thousands of kilometers away could survive this

77
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:41,040
journey.

78
00:02:41,390 --> 00:02:42,982
And for thousands of years,

79
00:02:42,982 --> 00:02:45,541
the river did something even more important.

80
00:02:45,541 --> 00:02:46,338
Every summer,

81
00:02:46,338 --> 00:02:47,020
it flooded.

82
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:50,105
From around July to September,

83
00:02:50,105 --> 00:02:50,302
the river rose and spread across the valley.

84
00:02:50,302 --> 00:02:50,424
When the water pulled back,

85
00:02:50,424 --> 00:02:50,563
it left behind a layer of dark,

86
00:02:50,563 --> 00:02:50,620
fertile mud.

87
00:02:50,970 --> 00:02:54,103
Ancient Egyptians called their country Kemet,

88
00:02:54,103 --> 00:02:55,193
the black land,

89
00:02:55,193 --> 00:02:56,283
after that mud.

90
00:02:56,283 --> 00:02:58,598
The desert around it was Deshret,

91
00:02:58,598 --> 00:02:59,552
the red land.

92
00:02:59,552 --> 00:03:00,778
Life on one side.

93
00:03:00,778 --> 00:03:02,140
Death on the other.

94
00:03:02,490 --> 00:03:05,540
Their whole calendar followed the river.

95
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:06,656
Three seasons:

96
00:03:06,656 --> 00:03:07,177
Akhet,

97
00:03:07,177 --> 00:03:07,996
the flood.

98
00:03:07,996 --> 00:03:08,516
Peret,

99
00:03:08,516 --> 00:03:10,004
the growing season.

100
00:03:10,004 --> 00:03:10,823
And Shemu,

101
00:03:10,823 --> 00:03:11,790
the harvest.

102
00:03:12,140 --> 00:03:13,975
They even built nilometers,

103
00:03:13,975 --> 00:03:17,251
stone staircases and wells that measured how high

104
00:03:17,251 --> 00:03:18,299
the flood rose.

105
00:03:18,299 --> 00:03:19,741
The higher the water,

106
00:03:19,741 --> 00:03:21,313
the bigger the harvest,

107
00:03:21,313 --> 00:03:24,000
and the more tax farmers paid that year.

108
00:03:24,350 --> 00:03:27,541
The Greek historian Herodotus summed it up about

109
00:03:27,541 --> 00:03:29,820
two and a half thousand years ago.

110
00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:30,276
Egypt,

111
00:03:30,276 --> 00:03:30,927
he wrote,

112
00:03:30,927 --> 00:03:32,620
is the gift of the river.

113
00:03:32,970 --> 00:03:35,448
Now look where the great monuments are.

114
00:03:35,448 --> 00:03:36,810
The pyramids of Giza.

115
00:03:36,810 --> 00:03:38,173
The temples of Luxor.

116
00:03:38,173 --> 00:03:38,607
Aswan.

117
00:03:38,607 --> 00:03:40,899
Every one of them sits right next to

118
00:03:40,899 --> 00:03:41,580
the river.

119
00:03:41,930 --> 00:03:44,220
The Nile was also Egypt's highway,

120
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:45,594
with a clever trick.

121
00:03:45,594 --> 00:03:47,229
The current flows north,

122
00:03:47,229 --> 00:03:50,043
but the wind usually blows from the north.

123
00:03:50,043 --> 00:03:53,052
So boats drifted downstream with the current,

124
00:03:53,052 --> 00:03:55,734
then raised their sails and let the wind

125
00:03:55,734 --> 00:03:57,370
push them back upstream.

126
00:03:57,720 --> 00:03:59,915
Even their writing reflects it.

127
00:03:59,915 --> 00:04:01,013
In hieroglyphs,

128
00:04:01,013 --> 00:04:03,894
the sign for traveling south shows a boat

129
00:04:03,894 --> 00:04:05,129
with its sail up.

130
00:04:05,129 --> 00:04:06,295
Traveling north,

131
00:04:06,295 --> 00:04:07,530
the sail is down.

132
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:10,341
And the flood may even help explain the

133
00:04:10,341 --> 00:04:10,956
pyramids.

134
00:04:10,956 --> 00:04:14,033
Many historians think that during the months when

135
00:04:14,033 --> 00:04:15,755
the fields were underwater,

136
00:04:15,755 --> 00:04:18,401
farmers were free to work on the pharaohs'

137
00:04:18,401 --> 00:04:19,570
building projects.

138
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:21,887
Reliable food from the river,

139
00:04:21,887 --> 00:04:24,837
and deserts on every side that kept invaders

140
00:04:24,837 --> 00:04:25,231
away.

141
00:04:25,231 --> 00:04:27,919
That combination is a big reason why one

142
00:04:27,919 --> 00:04:31,132
of the world's first great states appeared here,

143
00:04:31,132 --> 00:04:33,230
around five thousand years ago.

144
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:35,202
If you're enjoying this,

145
00:04:35,202 --> 00:04:35,916
subscribe.

146
00:04:35,916 --> 00:04:38,446
Every week we find the story hidden in

147
00:04:38,446 --> 00:04:39,290
another map.

148
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:44,907
But the flood was never predictable.

149
00:04:44,907 --> 00:04:47,142
Too little water meant hunger.

150
00:04:47,142 --> 00:04:50,170
Too much could wash entire villages away.

151
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:52,571
So in the twentieth century,

152
00:04:52,571 --> 00:04:55,683
Egypt decided to control the river once and

153
00:04:55,683 --> 00:04:56,320
for all.

154
00:04:56,670 --> 00:04:57,321
In 1970,

155
00:04:57,321 --> 00:04:59,781
the Aswan High Dam was completed.

156
00:04:59,781 --> 00:05:01,806
Behind it grew Lake Nasser,

157
00:05:01,806 --> 00:05:04,917
one of the largest artificial lakes in the

158
00:05:04,917 --> 00:05:05,423
world,

159
00:05:05,423 --> 00:05:08,100
around five hundred kilometers long.

160
00:05:08,450 --> 00:05:11,308
The new lake was going to drown ancient

161
00:05:11,308 --> 00:05:12,094
monuments.

162
00:05:12,094 --> 00:05:13,309
So in the 1960s,

163
00:05:13,309 --> 00:05:16,739
an international campaign led by UNESCO cut the

164
00:05:16,739 --> 00:05:19,812
giant temples of Abu Simbel into more than

165
00:05:19,812 --> 00:05:23,028
a thousand blocks and rebuilt them on higher

166
00:05:23,028 --> 00:05:23,599
ground,

167
00:05:23,599 --> 00:05:25,600
about sixty-five meters up.

168
00:05:25,950 --> 00:05:28,288
It wasn't the first dam at Aswan.

169
00:05:28,288 --> 00:05:30,764
A smaller one had been built by the

170
00:05:30,764 --> 00:05:31,933
British in 1902.

171
00:05:31,933 --> 00:05:34,408
But the new dam was on a completely

172
00:05:34,408 --> 00:05:35,577
different scale,

173
00:05:35,577 --> 00:05:37,640
and it changed Egypt forever.

174
00:05:37,990 --> 00:05:39,751
The dam ended the floods.

175
00:05:39,751 --> 00:05:41,851
It stored water for dry years,

176
00:05:41,851 --> 00:05:44,900
and it produced huge amounts of electricity.

177
00:05:45,250 --> 00:05:46,833
But there was a price.

178
00:05:46,833 --> 00:05:49,862
The fertile mud now gets trapped behind the

179
00:05:49,862 --> 00:05:50,206
dam.

180
00:05:50,206 --> 00:05:52,753
Farmers rely on fertilizers instead,

181
00:05:52,753 --> 00:05:54,680
and without fresh sediment,

182
00:05:54,680 --> 00:05:57,846
parts of the Nile Delta are slowly retreating

183
00:05:57,846 --> 00:05:58,810
into the sea.

184
00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:02,193
Today,

185
00:06:02,193 --> 00:06:04,481
the delta and the valley hold one of

186
00:06:04,481 --> 00:06:06,274
the biggest cities on Earth.

187
00:06:06,274 --> 00:06:08,871
Greater Cairo is home to more than twenty

188
00:06:08,871 --> 00:06:09,860
million people.

189
00:06:10,210 --> 00:06:13,969
There's one more thin strip where Egyptians cluster:

190
00:06:13,969 --> 00:06:15,530
along the Suez Canal.

191
00:06:15,530 --> 00:06:16,664
Opened in 1869,

192
00:06:16,664 --> 00:06:19,714
it links the Mediterranean to the Red Sea,

193
00:06:19,714 --> 00:06:22,339
and a big share of the world's trade

194
00:06:22,339 --> 00:06:23,686
passes through it,

195
00:06:23,686 --> 00:06:27,020
bringing Egypt billions of dollars every year.

196
00:06:27,370 --> 00:06:29,852
But the delta faces a new threat.

197
00:06:29,852 --> 00:06:32,187
Much of it lies only a meter or

198
00:06:32,187 --> 00:06:33,574
two above the sea,

199
00:06:33,574 --> 00:06:36,202
and as sea levels rise and the land

200
00:06:36,202 --> 00:06:37,224
slowly sinks,

201
00:06:37,224 --> 00:06:40,435
salt water is creeping into some of Egypt's

202
00:06:40,435 --> 00:06:41,530
best farmland.

203
00:06:41,880 --> 00:06:43,691
And Egypt keeps growing.

204
00:06:43,691 --> 00:06:47,676
Its population has multiplied roughly five times since

205
00:06:47,676 --> 00:06:48,111
1950,

206
00:06:48,111 --> 00:06:50,864
and almost all of that growth has had

207
00:06:50,864 --> 00:06:53,400
to fit into the same narrow strip.

208
00:06:53,750 --> 00:06:56,608
And the river itself is under pressure.

209
00:06:56,608 --> 00:06:57,322
Upstream,

210
00:06:57,322 --> 00:07:01,251
Ethiopia has built Africa's largest hydroelectric dam,

211
00:07:01,251 --> 00:07:03,894
the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam,

212
00:07:03,894 --> 00:07:05,180
on the Blue Nile.

213
00:07:05,530 --> 00:07:06,255
For Egypt,

214
00:07:06,255 --> 00:07:09,089
which gets more than ninety percent of its

215
00:07:09,089 --> 00:07:10,869
fresh water from the Nile,

216
00:07:10,869 --> 00:07:12,253
that's a huge worry.

217
00:07:12,253 --> 00:07:13,175
For Ethiopia,

218
00:07:13,175 --> 00:07:16,471
the dam means electricity for millions of people.

219
00:07:16,471 --> 00:07:19,173
Both countries see the river as a matter

220
00:07:19,173 --> 00:07:20,030
of survival.

221
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:22,863
Egypt is also trying to break out of

222
00:07:22,863 --> 00:07:23,668
the valley.

223
00:07:23,668 --> 00:07:24,675
East of Cairo,

224
00:07:24,675 --> 00:07:27,493
it's building a brand new capital city in

225
00:07:27,493 --> 00:07:28,298
the desert,

226
00:07:28,298 --> 00:07:30,982
and it's pumping Nile water to farm new

227
00:07:30,982 --> 00:07:32,660
land far from the river.

228
00:07:33,010 --> 00:07:36,335
So why do ninety-five percent of Egyptians live

229
00:07:36,335 --> 00:07:38,344
on five percent of the land?

230
00:07:38,344 --> 00:07:41,323
Because in a country where it almost never

231
00:07:41,323 --> 00:07:41,808
rains,

232
00:07:41,808 --> 00:07:44,232
the Nile is the only water you can

233
00:07:44,232 --> 00:07:44,925
count on.

234
00:07:44,925 --> 00:07:46,657
For five thousand years,

235
00:07:46,657 --> 00:07:48,389
wherever the river goes,

236
00:07:48,389 --> 00:07:49,220
Egypt goes.

237
00:07:49,570 --> 00:07:55,057
And Egypt isn't the only country shaped by

238
00:07:55,057 --> 00:07:57,991
where people can live.

239
00:07:57,991 --> 00:07:58,757
Next,

240
00:07:58,757 --> 00:08:04,626
we'll find out why most Canadians live within

241
00:08:04,626 --> 00:08:09,220
a short drive of the United States.
